{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {We have discussed filtering finite
 length sequences with finite length impulse
 response (FIR) filters. We have stressed
 the importance of the DFT method of computing
 the filtered output and compared the aperiodic
 and periodic convolutions. The filter was
 alternatively characterized by the N-periodic
 frequency response, which }{ }{was the 
DFT, of length N, of the FIR.}}
}
.TXT 11 0 284 0
Cg a60.875000,62.000000,322
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {In applications, commonly the input
 sequence is of an indefinite and long length
 (N). In this case it is reasonable to try
 letting N become indefinitely large. We
 shall see that the N-periodic frequency
 response has a well-defined limiting form
 which is a natural description of the FIR
 filter for such applications.}}
}
.TXT 9 0 302 0
Cg a53.750000,61.000000,84
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {We carry along in the discussion
 }{a specific illustration, an FIR of length
 M}{.}}
}
.EQN 3 7 2 0
{0:N}NAME:64
.EQN 0 10 3 0
{0:n}NAME:0;{0:N}NAME-1
.EQN 0 15 5 0
{0:M}NAME:8
.EQN 0 11 6 0
{0:m}NAME:0;{0:M}NAME-1
.EQN 4 -26 7 0
({0:h}NAME)[({0:n}NAME):{0:if}NAME({0:n}NAME<{0:M}NAME,(1)/({0:M}NAME),0)
