\i  }{\b \i0 \f0 ZT}{\i0 \f0 ,}{\f0  and
 is defined by (6.3) with exp(i}{\f1 w}{\f0 
n) replaced by z. In the DSP context H(z)
 is called the }{\b \i0 \f0 system function}{\b0 
\f0 \i0 ,}{\f0 \b \i0  SF}{\f0 . Since, 
for FIR filters, the only pole is at the
 origin, H(z) is an analytic function for
 all z not equal to 0. FIR filters are sometimes
 called, misleadingly, all-zero filters.}}
}
