{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {The phase of the frequency response
 is seen to contain 10 jumps of }{\f1 p}{:
 these are seen to be at the locations of
 sign changes in the two real sin( )/sin(
 ) factors. Recall that, by definition, 
the magnitude of a complex number is real
 and nonnegative; thus the sign changes 
in these factors must be effected by }{\f1 
p}{ changes in the }{phase. }}
}
.TXT 11 0 209 0
Cg a58.625000,61.125000,229
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {Compared to the simple MA filter,
 the 5 by 7 filter's IR has a }{\b \i0 taper}{:
 this is the feature that results in the
 lower gain outside the passband as well
 as the broader passband. We shall tapers
 in well-designed FIRs.}}
}
.TXT 7 0 220 0
Cg a61.500000,61.125000,515
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {\b Remark}{  It is important to
 note that, if we have available the frequency
 response       H1(}{\f1 w}{), defined as
 the DTFT of the FIR, we can obtain }{\ulnone 
any}{ N-periodic frequency          response
 simply by }{\b \i0 \ulnone sampling}{ the
 frequency response at the points \{}{\f1 
w}{\dn8 k}{ = (2}{\f1 p}{/N)k,   k = 0,...,N-1\}.
 Conversely, the N-periodic frequency response
 of an FIR filter (of          length M<=N)
 is always a continuous function of a variable
 }{\f1 w}{ replacing (2}{\f1 p}{/N)k. }}
}
.TXT 12 0 221 0
Cg a59.875000,61.000000,336
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {Since it is usually true in practice
 that the FIR filter length M is short relative
 to the data length N and that the latter
 may vary in specific applications of the
 filter, it is common practice to specify
 the FIR filter by a frequency response 
defined as the DTFT. The usual design procedures
 approximate a selected desideratum.}}
}
