{\plain {\b Problem}{\b \ulnone \f0  6.2}{\f0 
  Vary K in Example 6.3 and explain the 
results. Plot the frequency       response,
 in particular its phase: what distinguishes
 it from the previous examples,   especially
 the MA filter's frequency response phase
 ?}}
}
.TXT 8 0 374 0
Cg a58.375000,61.000000,396
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {The inverse DTFT, as an integral
 over a finite region, could be evaluated,
 for each value of n of interest, using 
standard numerical integration algorithms
 present in mathematical software such as
 Mathcad. However, we have already noted
 that }{ computing }{the IDTFT }{by this
 method}{ takes a long time.}{ Again, the
 IDFT is much more likely to be implemented
 in any practical DSP system.}}
}
.TXT 12 0 375 0
Cg a61.500000,62.000000,464
{\rtf1\ansi \deff0
{\fonttbl
{\f0\fnil Arial;}
{\f1\fnil Symbol;}
{\f2\fnil CG Times;}
}
{\plain {Finally, t}{he IDTFT}{ (6.9b) can
 be evaluated using}{ integration in the
 complex z-plane. As the integrand is a 
function of }{\f1 w}{ only as }{exp(i}{\f1 
w}{)}{, we substitute z = }{exp(i}{\f1 w}{)}{.
 Note that dz = i}{\f1 w}{ }{exp(i}{\f1 
w}{)}{ d}{\f1 w}{ so that d}{\f1 w}{ = iz}{\fs16 
\up6 -1}{dz}{. As }{\f1 w}{ varies through
 2}{\f1 p}{ radians, z travels once around
 the unit circle in the complex plane. Thus
 (6.9) becomes the }{\b contour integral}}
}
